<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Overall&nbsp;critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm not feeling this for a few reasons. I'll go in-depth but it's mainly due to the narrative, structure, and dialogue.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>Narrative</strong></span><br />
The issue here is that you open a few threads and never tie them up. You give us XXXX's overconfidence and obliviousness, XXXX's relationship with the scarecrow and Lana, and SCP-XXXX's cowboy tendencies and how they interact in the modern world. You set these up but never finish them.</p>
<p>For the overconfidence/obliviousness, we're given two addenda and neither are <em>that</em> funny. It's also not used to forward the narrative in any meaningful way other than XXXX not understanding something and doing it wrong. It manages to drive a car and crashes it, it thinks a fire hydrant is a cactus, it breaks a thermostat, so on. None of these comedic beats/characterization moments should both connect me to XXXX and forward the plot, they neither in the long run. The cowboy trait of XXXX is also quickly glossed over after it loses the horse. By the end, I just read about an idiotic cowboy cactus and am left saying &quot;so what&quot;</p>
<p>XXXX's relationship with the scarecrow and Lana is left at what I could barely describe as a cliffhanger. Lana says pretty much the same thing about her shop four times in a row and doesn't give us any new information to make me understand the connection. There's also the fact that I have nothing but a vague idea of what this rivalry is about. Develop this further, this is where you will bridge the characters to the reader. Focus on this.</p>
<p>SCP-XXXX's cowboy tendencies basically boil down to it shoots things. There's so much you can do with a fish-out-of-water character that isn't tapped into here.</p>
<p>Overall, there is a lack of connection between all the addendums, they make it so I am unable to connect with the characters along with the fact that there aren't many comedic moments. My advice, focus on SCP-XXXX's connection with Lana and the Scarecrow, explain to us why there's this rivalry, perhaps make the Scarecrow the straightman against the Cactus idiot, and/or explain their origins. It feels like you're trying many different forms of humor, there's absurdist with the explosions, set-up-punchline in the dialogue, slapstick with the fight, etc. You're speaking yourself thin.</p>
<p>A specific thing I'd like to note is that explosions for the sake of explosions feel both unwarranted and not too funny.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>Structure</strong></span><br />
This one is short and will probably end up being unneeded if you take my above advice. The third addendum is out of place and should be combined with either the discovery section or the beginning of the first addendum.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>Dialogue</strong></span><br />
This is going to be the make or break factor of this article. Natural dialogue is a bit tricky so I will point out some examples where my suspension of disbelief was broken:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If I can get a carriage that doesn't need no horse, then I can go fast whenever and wherever I need! Ain't even gotta water it or pick up its doo-doo!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Both unfunny and unnatural</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You should be worrying more about your own safety first! In fact, why are you in a shack in the middle of the Chihuahuan Desert? &lt;Looks past elderly woman to see trinkets and souvenirs on display.&gt; Wait, did you say 'shop'?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Feels like you were going for the stating-the-obvious form of humor, this would be better if left as a quirk of the anomaly, or change the location altogether.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No, dearie. You're not my grandson. You're fired.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Confusing because of the lack of backstory.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Oh, I'd love to purchase this guy!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think people talk like this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That's the nicest thing anybody's ever said about me! Shoot, my old lady Lana can't even call me that anymore! 'cause she went blind!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not funny and a bit odd.</p>
<p>A rewrite of problematic dialogue will probably come with the addition of my proposed changes. Remember to speak the lines out loud, if you can't imagine yourself saying it, even if you are a cowboy cactus, it should be rewritten or cut.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

