<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In fairness, most of this is fairly nitpicky - take that for what you will.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX's other anomalous properties include mild telekinesis that mimics the usage, length and approximate strength of two human arms, the ability to levitate 2 meters in the air and 'swing' itself at its opponents.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm an Oxford comma fan - I read this as &quot;(mild&#8230;arms), (the ability&#8230;opponents),&quot; meaning it's missing a conjunction. Or, if levitation and swinging are two separate traits, a comma after &quot;air.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX was initially seen in the neighborhood of Sprucelude Acres in <strong>Hollister</strong>, Texas<br />
It then traveled 6 kilometers across the city of <strong>Holston</strong>, Texas</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm guessing the second is also supposed to be Hollister?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&lt;Leaps onto counter, scattering specks of soil onto counter.&gt;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Counter twice here just reads kinda clunky to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It later fell asleep. The following morning, a customer attempted to purchase SCP-XXXX. It noticed SCP-XXXX-1 embedded in the soil in its pot and, believing it was a toy, pulled the trigger</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I had to read this twice before I understood (I think) that the customer is the one pulling the trigger? Or did the cactus somehow acquire the anomalous gun while sleeping? OR, popping back up here after I made it to the bottom again, did the cactus just not notice the gun until that point, akin to not noticing the picture was sticking out of its pot? I just don't know.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It rode the horse 194,121 meters</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Super specific. You use kilometers elsewhere in the article, is there any particular importance of these numbers? It doesn't seem to fit to switch to meters here, especially since it's quite the distance.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It spots Lana Ringle and the scarecrow</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We've yet to be introduced to this scarecrow. Following the log, this makes sense, but it reads weird to me to just call it &quot;the scarecrow&quot; at this point. It's like it's assumptive that we should already know what this is.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Opinions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This is one of those that just doesn't vibe with me. I've seen plenty of similar articles (by way of tone and humor, that is), and they clearly work. I'm just very humorless as a rule. I totally see what you're going for, and you hit the bullseye; I'm just not the right target.</p>
<p>That being said, I do see how it <em>could</em> hit well. I'm hesitant to say a whole lot, honestly, because humor is so subjective. I'd personally call it &quot;cute,&quot; or &quot;kitsch&quot; (but without all the negative connotations I wasn't aware of until I googled how to spell that properly). But, if others <em>have</em> said that it's funny, I'd trust them in that regard.</p>
<p>I'd give it a +1 for meeting my arbitrary criteria, but it also wouldn't be one of the articles I'd be excited about, or keen on linking to other people expressly to promote it.</p>
</div>
</div>
