<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[Placeholder]</span></p>
<p>Just a short foreword: I'm super bad at line-by-line because living with non-native speakers have allowed me to basically ignore most SPaG errors, so I will only point them out if they are quite jarring. Phrasing-wise too, I'll be unable to help extensively due to the same reason mentioned above.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The temperature to its containment chamber</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'To' change to 'in'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX's other anomalous properties include mild telekinesis that mimics the usage, length and approximate strength of two human arms,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hmm, I might just be nitpicking here, but psychokinesis would be much more suited here. Also, remove the mild, I don't think it adds much.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is capable of firing ammunition customary of non-anomalous revolvers,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Customary just sounds plain weird. Maybe 'It is capable of firing regular revolver ammunition' would work better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX was initially seen in the neighborhood of Sprucelude Acres in Hollister, Texas after a civilian woman was kidnapped by a humanoid assailant, later identified to be an anomalous scarecrow, and forced into a vehicle.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think just a little too much happens here. The article would probably flow better if the anomalous scarecrow was introduced a little more subtly, but I'm unsure how I can help with that. It's just a lot to take in, you know? It's a straight up exposition and we have to take the plot at face-value, which detracts from the mystery quite a bit. Leading the readers on something similar to a wild goose chase along with the cactus only to find out the scarecrow is the perpetrator at the end might make the story much better in terms of structure.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>ejects an unidentified fluid from the portion of its body that's in between its arms. This fluid lands in the basket that holds plastic Starbucks branded cups for sale.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's weird, what was that meant to do?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It then proceeds to repeatedly slam into the door</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Clarifying it as glass might make it a tad bit funnier.</p>
<p>I had to google what succulent section meant. Please make it clearer by saying 'Succulent Plants section' instead, that might make it less confusing for readers with no prior knowledge of stuff like this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Scrankle Sale Barn in Hollister, Texas where it illegally obtained a horse</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Add a comma after the Texas.</p>
<p>And&#8230; I'm done reading it.</p>
<p>Hmm, I feel like the interaction between Lana, Scarecrow and Cactus could have been better. It's like, you built the suspense well and I was quite interested to see where it led, but everything was stated so plainly that I didn't really feel satisfied with it. If there was a more subtle introduction of mystery and the big 'Bam! They're just toys that are jealous!' reveal, it would be much more elegant. However, although I did feel the tension, I wasn't really worried for Lana? Most of the concern is coming from Prixor here and we have to just take it because that's all that's stated outright.</p>
<p>The only thing this piece suffers from is admittedly, the short word count for what could be a great narrative. You decided to go with a give-away-all-the-plot-through-ramblings-of-the-characters approach and it bogs down the rest of the story. What you need is more danger, more wacky stuff (we're talking about a pinata and doll that turned into enemies here!), and more subtle writing.</p>
<p>Moving on, the last log didn't really do much for me? It didn't add anything so I think you can just outright remove it, or just revamp it so that it flows into the next part of the story, maybe.</p>
<p>Overall, kinda funny, but still not ready for the mainsite.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

